      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The majority opinion in *Dobbs v. Jackson* concluded that the Constitution does not confer a right to abortion, overturning the precedent established in *Roe v. Wade* and *Planned Parenthood v. Casey*. The Court reasoned that the right to abortion is not explicitly listed in the Bill of Rights and that the concept of \"undoubtedly, but also necessarily, ambiguous\" rights found in the Due Process Clause no longer extends to abortion after over 50 years of settled law. Conversely, the dissent argues the ruling is constitutionally flawed because it abandons the \"mistake of fact\" argument regarding *Roe*'s 1973 analysis, as the Court already acknowledged in *Casey* that the trimester framework was factually incorrect and should have been overruled by *Casey* itself.\n\nBy ignoring these early admissions and instead grounding the decision in \"normal clear text and common understanding,\" the dissent claims the majority is acting unilaterally and undemocratically. Furthermore, critics argue the opinion disproves the value of precedent in constitutional law by effectively erasing 50 years of established judicial doctrine without a constitutional amendment. While the majority feels compelled to correct a decades-long error, the",
